course: dictionary
lesson: 3179
title: The small word en
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «En» is not a normal English word. You will not find it on its own in an English sentence. It comes from French, and you will meet it in French lessons, in a few borrowed phrases, and in printing.
explain: In French, «en» usually means «in» or «to». It is used with most feminine countries, with seasons and with some ways of travelling: «en Italie» (in Italy), «en été» (in summer), «en bus» (by bus). If a sentence is in French, look at the words around «en» to work out its job.
explain: English borrows a few phrases with «en». «The fans arrived en masse» means they all came together in a big group. «I am en route to school» means I am on the way. Learn the whole phrase, not just the single word.
explain: In typing, the en dash (–) is a dash about the width of the letter n. Use it for number ranges, like «pages 4–9». It is longer than a hyphen (-).
fact: «En» is a French word meaning «in», «to», or «of it».
fact: «En» is used before most feminine countries, such as «en Italie» and «en Allemagne».
fact: «En» also means «some» in French: «j'en veux» means «I want some».
fact: «En masse» means all together in a big group, and «en route» means on the way.
fact: The en dash (–) is about the width of the letter n, and it is used for number ranges.
exercise: In the phrase «the fans arrived en masse», what does en masse mean? | Quietly, one at a time | *All together in a big group | In a hurry for the bus | Late at night
exercise: Which dash is used for number ranges like «pages 4–9»? | A full stop (.) | *The en dash (–), about the width of the letter n | A question mark (?) | A comma (,)
exercise: Which French phrase means «in Italy»? | *«en Italie» | «à Italie» | «au Italie» | «aux Italie»
test: In «j'en veux», what does «en» mean? | On the way | Dangerous | Very hungry | *Some, or of it
test: What does «en route» mean? | In summer | Very hungry | Lost in the woods | *On the way
exam: What is «en» best described as? | *A French word meaning «in», «to» or «of it», not an English word on its own | The name of a French town | A word that always means dangerous | A type of dash only
